condition_name: Diabetes in Pregnancy
condition_slug: diabetes-in-pregnancy
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is any degree of glucose intolerance first recognised in pregnancy. If inadequately managed, GDM is associated with increased risk of perinatal morbidity and mortality. Diagnosis and prompt institution of therapy reduce the risks of poor outcomes."
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: When to screen for GDM
    features[9]: Between 24 and 28 weeks of gestation,Women with high risk for GDM,BMI 25 kg/m²,Previous history of GDM,Glycosuria,Previous large baby (> 4 kg),Poor obstetric history,Family history of diabetes,Known IGT / IFG
investigations[3]: Daily SBGM (urine glucose monitoring) is not useful in pregnancy,Regular assessment of maternal wellbeing should include blood pressure and urine protein,Regular surveillance for foetal well-being
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[9]: "Combined health care team including obstetrician, diabetologist, diabetes educator, and paediatrician/neonatologist",Initial therapy is dietary modification,Spread carbohydrate over 3 small to moderate sized meals and 2 - 3 snacks/day,Consider an evening snack to prevent starvation ketosis,Energy intake should provide for desirable weight gain during pregnancy,For obese women a 30 - 33% calorie restriction is advised,Delivery at 38 weeks gestation recommended,Withdraw therapy for diabetes after birth,Re-assess classification of maternal status at 6 weeks post partum
  drug[2]: "Initiate insulin therapy if fasting plasma glucose is > 5.8 mmol/L, 1 hour post-prandial glucose is > 8.6 mmol/L, 2 hour post-prandial plasma glucose is >7.5 mmol/L",Modify insulin regimen to achieve above targets
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[5]: Stroke,Seizures,Trauma,Drug overdose,Ethanol intoxication
complications[1]: Acute metabolic complications of diabetes mellitus
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null